WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:05.270
Horror movies are full of shocking violence,
terrifying monsters and, occasionally hilarious

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.040
bloopers, like these favorites we showed you
last time around.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.400
[Screaming]

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.040
“You must be f---ing joking!"

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.200
“Over here, over here, over here!

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.340
Oh, bollocks!

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.480
Okay, just, just, just f--- off!

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.620
F--- off!”

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.420
[Screaming]

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.150
“I actually bit her, I’m sorry.”

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.000
But there are plenty more where those came
from!

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.550
Here's a look at some more bloopers that completely
change these horror movies.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:33.550
Fright Night

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:37.860
The defining characteristics of a vampire
have developed over more than a century of

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:38.940
fiction and film.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.730
Everybody knows they sleep in coffins, go
out only at night, and have big fangs they

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.830
use to puncture their victims' necks to more
effectively drink their blood.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:50.820
Countless vampire movies have featured scenes
of vampires dramatically exposing those chompers,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.190
but it loses its dramatic effect and descends
into silliness if, say, those fangs were to

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:59.250
spontaneously fall out of an actor's mouth—as
Christopher Mintz-Plasse discovered while

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.550
filming this scene for the 2011 Fright Night
remake.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.360
“Seriously, stay away - oh, f---.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.420
My fang fell out.”

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:10.980
Warm Bodies

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.350
Part horror, part comedy, Warm Bodies concerns
a zombified young man stumbling around and

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:19.380
falling in love—and a kid's gotta eat, so
there are plenty of zombie-on-human attack

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.380
scenes.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.980
Here, star Nicholas Hoult is supposed to be
going after some human prey, but he surprises

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.160
his co-star and crew with some goofing off,
unleashing a loud, boisterous impression of

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.700
one of the backwoods torturers from Deliverance—a
very different kind of horror movie.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.820
“It’s mine, m-----------.”

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.240
[Scream]
“Squeal like a pig, boy!”

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.240
[Laughter]

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.060
Hostel: Part II

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.770
Eli Roth's second Hostel is maybe even more
gruesome than the first.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:50.730
So many disturbing things go down: murder,
torture, power saws used as weapons, soccer

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.150
played with a human head, and all kinds of
the gory stuff that makes going to the movies

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.020
such a magical experience.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.930
And yet the most unnerving thing to ever go
down in the movie, the moment that will linger

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:04.730
with viewers for years to come, is this disturbingly
funny outtake from Hostel: Part II where the

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:07.159
victims don't seem so sad after all:

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:12.110
“This man will show you the way.”

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.110
Disturbia

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.620
The terror of seeing of a dead body is kind
of at the center of the entire horror genre.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.980
But if you're an actor, seeing festering,
decaying bodies—either dummies or actors

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.940
in make-up—day in and day out takes the
sting out of it.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.380
So really, when a snickering Shia LeBeouf
approaches some monstrosity on the set of

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.400
Disturbia and kisses it, he's just trying
to jazz up his workday.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.340
[Laughter]
“And, cut!”

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.610
Young Frankenstein

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.610
Finally, the Mel Brooks classic Young Frankenstein
isn't a horror movie itself, but it does perfectly

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.910
skewer old Hollywood horror movies.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.930
Comedian Marty Feldman is one of the film's
many bright spots, portraying Dr. Frankenstein's

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:56.319
faithful assistant Igor as some strange combination
of maniac, pet, child, creep, and vaudeville

00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:57.319
performer.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:01.380
All those parts come into play in equal measure
during this scene, when Feldman gets a little

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.640
too carried away and tears apart a prop with
his teeth.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.980
[Growling]
“Stop it!”

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.040
“It’s gonna be a long night, so if you
need any help with the girls, don’t hesitate-”

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.190
Thanks for watching!

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:15.709
